# 006 — one cart yields at most one order group.
#
# Three files, one concern — the guard cannot work without all three:
#  * repository: `OrderGroupRepository.findAndCount` is a raw-SQL override that
#    recognises only id / customer_id / seller_id / status / sales_channel_id /
#    created_at / updated_at / q. A `cart_id` filter was silently DROPPED, so a
#    by-cart lookup returned the whole table. `cart_id` is now honoured.
#  * workflow: the `order_group` lookup selected `fields: ["cart_id"]`, so
#    `orderGroup?.data?.id` was `undefined` on every entry and the
#    `when(!orderGroupId)` create branch always ran. Selecting `id` too makes the
#    existing guard fire, so a racer past the route gets 200 carrying the
#    pre-existing group. Selecting `id` WITHOUT the repository clause is worse
#    than the bug: the guard would then match the newest group in the store and
#    silently skip order creation for every later cart.
#  * route: a cart with `completed_at` set now 409s before the workflow runs,
#    which is the cheap correct answer for a refreshed confirmation page.
#
# Neither the route nor the workflow layer alone is sufficient: `completed_at` is
# written inside the create branch, so five concurrent callers all read it unset.

